{"id":"speculative-decoding-and-token-verification","t":"Speculative decoding: verify draft tokens by rejection sampling","tags":["Inference","Language Models","Sampling"],"d":"Hard","rt":"numpy","fn":"verify","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"In speculative decoding a small **draft** model proposes $k$ tokens, then the large **target** model scores all of them in one forward pass. A rejection-sampling rule decides how many drafts to keep, and the result is distributed **exactly** as if the target had sampled every token itself.\n\nWrite `verify(draft, q, p, u_accept, u_sample)`.\n\n## Inputs\n- `draft`: int array `(k,)`, $k \\ge 1$, the proposed tokens $x_0, \\dots, x_{k-1}$.\n- `q`: float `(k, V)`: row $i$ is the draft model's distribution that $x_i$ was sampled from (so $q_i(x_i) > 0$).\n- `p`: float `(k + 1, V)`: row $i$ is the target's distribution at the same position; row $k$ is the target's distribution after all $k$ drafts.\n- `u_accept`: float `(k,)` and `u_sample`: float, uniform draws in $[0, 1)$.\n\n## Rule\nFor $i = 0, 1, \\dots$:\n1. **Accept** $x_i$ if `u_accept[i]` $< p_i(x_i) / q_i(x_i)$ (always true when $p_i(x_i) \\ge q_i(x_i)$). Append it and continue.\n2. Otherwise **reject**: stop, and sample one replacement token from the residual distribution $r = \\max(p_i - q_i, 0) / \\sum \\max(p_i - q_i, 0)$.\n\nIf all $k$ drafts are accepted, sample one **bonus** token from $p_k$. Either way exactly one sampled token is appended, so a call produces between $1$ and $k + 1$ tokens.\n\n**Sampling from a distribution $w$ with `u_sample`:** the smallest index $j$ with $\\sum_{l \\le j} w_l >$ `u_sample`; if rounding leaves none, the largest index with $w_j > 0$.\n\n## Output\nA tuple `(tokens, n_accepted, alpha)`:\n- `tokens`: list of Python ints, the accepted drafts followed by the sampled token.\n- `n_accepted`: int, the number of drafts accepted.\n- `alpha`: float array `(k,)`, the acceptance probability of each position, $\\alpha_i = \\sum_x \\min(p_i(x), q_i(x))$ (the chance a fresh draft at position $i$ survives).","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef verify(draft, q, p, u_accept, u_sample):\n    \"\"\"Return (tokens list, n_accepted int, alpha (k,) array).\"\"\"\n    k = len(draft)\n    return [int(t) for t in draft], k, np.ones(k)       # accepts everything, no bonus token\n","hints":["Walk the drafts in order and stop at the first rejection. Accepting when `u < p/q` is the same as accepting with probability $\\min(1, p/q)$.","On rejection the replacement comes from $\\max(p_i - q_i, 0)$, renormalised: exactly the probability mass the draft under-proposed. It is never all zero, because a rejection implies $q_i(x_i) > p_i(x_i)$.","If no draft is rejected, the target's last row $p_k$ is still unused: sample the bonus token from it. `alpha` is just `np.minimum(p[:k], q).sum(axis=1)`."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef _sample(w, u):\n    c = np.cumsum(w)\n    hit = np.nonzero(c > u)[0]\n    return int(hit[0]) if len(hit) else int(np.nonzero(w > 0)[0][-1])\n\ndef verify(draft, q, p, u_accept, u_sample):\n    k = len(draft)\n    alpha = np.minimum(p[:k], q).sum(axis=1)       # overlap of the two distributions per position\n    tokens = []\n    for i in range(k):\n        x = int(draft[i])\n        if u_accept[i] < p[i, x] / q[i, x]:         # accept with probability min(1, p/q)\n            tokens.append(x)\n            continue\n        resid = np.maximum(p[i] - q[i], 0.0)        # mass the target wants but the draft under-proposed\n        tokens.append(_sample(resid / resid.sum(), u_sample))\n        return tokens, i, alpha\n    tokens.append(_sample(p[k], u_sample))          # every draft accepted: free bonus token from the target\n    return tokens, k, alpha\n","ex_md":"## Why it works\nFor one position, the output token is $x$ with probability\n$$q(x)\\min\\!\\Bigl(1, \\tfrac{p(x)}{q(x)}\\Bigr) + \\Bigl(1 - \\sum_y \\min(p(y), q(y))\\Bigr)\\,\\frac{\\max(p(x) - q(x), 0)}{\\sum_y \\max(p(y) - q(y), 0)}.$$\nThe first term is $\\min(p, q)$. The two normalisers are both $1 - \\alpha$, so the second term is $\\max(p - q, 0)$. Their sum is $p(x)$: the output is an exact sample from the target, whatever the draft model is. A bad draft only lowers the acceptance rate, never the quality.\n\n## Speed\nWith per-position acceptance $\\alpha$ (assumed constant) the expected number of tokens per target pass is $(1 - \\alpha^{k+1}) / (1 - \\alpha)$. With $\\alpha = 0.8$ and $k = 4$ that is about 3.4 tokens for one target pass plus $k$ cheap draft passes. Decode is memory-bound, so verifying $k + 1$ positions costs roughly the same time as generating one: the win is large at low batch sizes and shrinks as the batch grows and the target becomes compute-bound.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Resampling from $p_i$ instead of the residual on rejection: the output is then biased toward tokens the draft likes.\n- Forgetting the bonus token when every draft is accepted, which wastes the target's last row.\n- Continuing after a rejection: later drafts were conditioned on a token that is no longer in the sequence.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- With greedy decoding, what does the rule reduce to? (Accept while the draft token equals the target argmax; replace the first mismatch with the target argmax.)\n- How do you pick $k$? (Balance draft cost against expected accepted length; adaptive schemes shrink $k$ when recent acceptance is low or the batch is large.)","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([0, 2])\narray([[0.5, 0.5, 0. ],\n       [0.2, 0.3, 0.5]])\narray([[0.5, 0.5, 0. ],\n       [0.2, 0.3, 0.5],\n       [0.1, 0.1, 0.8]])\narray([0.9, 0.9])\n0.75","out":"([0, 2, 2], 2, array([1., 1.]))"},{"in":"array([1, 0])\narray([[0.2, 0.8, 0. ],\n       [0.6, 0.2, 0.2]])\narray([[0.5, 0.5, 0. ],\n       [0.2, 0.3, 0.5],\n       [0.1, 0.1, 0.8]])\narray([0.5, 0.1])\n0.3","out":"([1, 0, 2], 2, array([0.7, 0.6]))"}],"lessons":[{"href":"../inference-eng-course/index.html#l16-03","t":"Acceptance, Rejection & Draft Length","c":"Inference Engineering"},{"href":"../inference-eng-course/index.html#l16-02","t":"Draft Models, Target Models & Parallel Verification","c":"Inference Engineering"}],"related":["text-generation-sampling","temperature-top-k-and-top-p-sampling","autoregressive-decoding-with-kv-cache"],"ntests":9,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [0, 2], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.5, 0.5, 0.0], [0.2, 0.3, 0.5]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.5, 0.5, 0.0], [0.2, 0.3, 0.5], [0.1, 0.1, 0.8]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.9, 0.9], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.75], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [[0, 2, 2], 2, {\"__nd__\": [1.0, 1.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [1, 0], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.2, 0.8, 0.0], [0.6, 0.2, 0.2]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.5, 0.5, 0.0], [0.2, 0.3, 0.5], [0.1, 0.1, 0.8]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.5, 0.1], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.3], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [[1, 0, 2], 2, {\"__nd__\": [0.7, 0.6000000000000001], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [1, 0], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.2, 0.8, 0.0], [0.6, 0.2, 0.2]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.5, 0.5, 0.0], [0.2, 0.3, 0.5], [0.1, 0.1, 0.8]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.7, 0.1], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.3], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [[0], 0, {\"__nd__\": [0.7, 0.6000000000000001], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [2], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.0, 0.0, 1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.0, 0.0, 1.0], [0.25, 0.25, 0.5]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.999], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.999999], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [[2, 2], 1, {\"__nd__\": [1.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [1, 5, 5, 3], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.10140209417448573, 0.5436242060033466, 0.06580963259455258, 0.052106284481530936, 0.08431797271237634, 0.1527398100337078], [0.21081564703058658, 0.09340037083668043, 0.043516560677108